  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{2}^{8}\\frac{{log}_{2}\u2061(2x)}{x}\\,\\text{d}x=($\n{{field-1}}\n$)\\operatorname{ln}\u20612$",
  "worked_solution": "Let $u={log}_{2}\u2061(2x).$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2x\\operatorname{ln}\u20612}\\cdot 2=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}\u20612}\\,\u27f9\\,\\operatorname{ln}\u20612\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{8}\\frac{{log}_{2}\u2061(2x)}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{2}^{8}{log}_{2}\u2061(2x)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{4}u\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}\u20612\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}\u20612{\\int }_{2}^{4}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}\u20612\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}{|}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}\u20612(16-4) \\\\  & =(6)\\operatorname{ln}\u20612\\end{aligned}\n$$",
